dren being  hospitalized  again mostly among the unvaccinated  and about vaccination just to refresh  your memory  the fisa bioentec is now authorized and  recommended for children from five to  seventeen  children from twelve to seventeen can  get a booster the fda authorized to  fifteen and as  he said  today the acip will meet about that for  recommendation  and finally a question that many ask and  i'm sure many of the reporters who have  children are asking this  what about kids from six months to five  years  those studies are ongoing now to get the  right dosage regimen to get to where we  want to be and likely the clinical trial  are ongoing and in in individuals less  than five years hopefully in the first  half  hopefully towards the earlier part of  the first half we may have some  information so that we can vaccinate  children of that age  i'll stop there and back to you jeff  well thank you doctors and with that  let's open it up for some questions  kevin  thanks jeff um we have a lot of  questions today please keep your  questions to one question we'll get  through as many as we can first let's go  to jeannie bauman at bloomberg  all right thank you so much for taking  my question um i know dr wolinski has  said part of the reason that um cdc is  not requiring a a test after five days  of isolation is that's not what the  chests are designed to do so i was just  wondering if there are plans by red x or  other programs to develop tests that  could actually detect you know how  infectious you are how contagious you  are thank you  dr wilson you want to clarify uh the  the yeah  so the fda rapid tests are authorized um  as you say genie for um  diagnosis they are not a quantitative  test they are qualitative tests to  diagnose they're they're authorized for  use in the first seven days of symptoms  um and uh they are authorized for you  serially um in things like test to stay  where you have a negative and an another  negative um you can confirm that people  continue to be negative um maybe i'll  pass it to dr tony uh dr fauci where uh  to talk about red x and what is going on  in the design of other tests for  qualitative quantitative purposes  yeah okay so obviously there's a lot of  work going on  in the uh bioimaging and bioengineering  institute looking at refining and  fine-tuning tests from every aspect from  sensitivity specificity and  predictability and those are ongoing  studies now with a considerable  investment on a number of investigators  thank you next  question let's go to zeke miller at the  ap  um thanks uh uh uh for for doing this  today uh for dr lolinski um i know  there's been a number of questions about  this over the last several months but  given the data you've uh discussed today  about the benefits of booster doses um  and dr factor's comments yesterday about  sort of potential changes to cdc policy  is the cdc going to change its  definition of booster doses or  and then for jeff for people who are not  fully up to date with their vaccination  they're being mean they're eligible but  haven't gotten a booster um  should they be expecting to see you some  you know that's some of the prohibitions  that people who are not fully vaccinated  you know whether it comes to travel or  employment with the federal government  that they would be subject to some of  those prohibitions down the line  dr malinsky first  yeah super thank you zeke so individuals  are considered fully vaccinated against  coven 19 if they've received their  primary series that definition is not  changing but consistent with how public  health has historically viewed or even  talked about how we recommend vaccines  we are now recommending that individuals  stay up to date with additional doses  that they are eligible for and we have  now available um how you can stay up to  date with your cova 19 vaccines based on  what vaccine you have received and what  age group you are in and that is  available now on the cdc website back to  you jeff so zeke to repeat what dr  wolinski just said someone is considered  fully vaccinated if they receive their  primary series of vaccines so if you  think about the different requirements i  think you mentioned travel  the osha and cms rules as other examples  that has not changed and we do not have  any plans to change that  i do think it's really important  to to recognize the vast majority of  hospitalizations and deaths are among  the unvaccinated americans and  completing the primary  vaccination series is clearly a critical  step to prevent severe outcomes with  boosters as dr malinsky said giving the  highest level of protection so we'll  encourage everybody to get vaccinated  and when eligible boosted as to as to  the definition uh someone is considered  fully vaccinated they've received their  primary series of vaccine next question  good jackie heinrich with fox news  thank you guys for doing this briefing  um i wanted to ask about the fda's um  recommendations that have come out since  november uh  and seemingly happening without  consulting uh the panel of independent  experts that were consulted for  uh approvals on the vaccines themselves  has there been a policy change or  uh  sort of a shift away from standard  practice when it comes to  recommendations for boosters versus the  process that everyone went through for  the vaccine itself  well i would defer to the fda and the  specific fda question though i do know  that  fda like cdc where i will ask dr  welinski to comment about her  reliance on the expertise of the asip  does consult with  its experts on a regular basis and  formally convenes them as you know uh  regularly dr malinsky you want to talk  about the cdc experience with your group  of outside experts in the asap yeah  absolutely and thank you for that  question jackie so obviously these are  unprecedented times where there's so  much action happening right now with  regard to vaccination and given those  unprecedented times we've convened our  advisory committee on immunization  practices more than ever before  we had a meeting prior to  the week prior to christmas and we are  now having a meeting today so the week  after new year's  and i am in regular touch in regular  touch with these experts um to ensure  that they are confident as to the  discussions that they feel like need to  be had as we're making decisions about  vaccination for the country  great next question please  go to jeremy diamond at cnn  hey thanks very much for doing this um  couple questions for dr wolanski um dr  wolinski last week you said that we  actually don't know how our rapid tests  perform and how well they predict  whether you're transmissible during the  end of disease but now the cdc guidance  says people who test positive on an  antigen test at day five should continue  to isolate for another five days so i'm  hoping you can explain which is it uh do  these tests serve a purpose to assess  transmissibility uh or not and secondly  uh amid the additions to the new  guidance yesterday uh you say that  people should avoid travel restaurants  and eating around others at home and at  work until 10 10 days  so why wasn't that information included  last week and how do you expect people  to keep track of what they can and can't  do  yeah thank you for that question jeremy  so um first i will say as noted before  the fda has authorized these rapid tests  for early in the disease course the  first seven days they've clarified that  or they've said that these tests are for  qualitative purposes not quantitative  purposes meaning we can't tell how  transmissible you are based on a  positive or negative test  um and that negatives um you know are  often most helpful when we're using them  serially multiple times in a row um that  said after we released our guidance uh  our recommendations uh early last week  it became very clear that people were  interested in using the rapid tests  though not authorized for this purpose  for this purpose after their end of  isolation period and because there was  um  interest in using them for this reason  we then provided guidance on how they  should be used so if one is to take an  extra step and  perform a test at the end of their  five-day isolation period we wanted to  make sure people understood how they  should be interpreted if that test is  positive people should stay home for  those extra five days  um and if that test is negative people  really do need to understand that they  must continue to wear their mask um for  those uh extra five days after um  to complete a 10-day isolation period um  back to you jeff  next question please  let's go to ahmed at reuters  hi yeah thank you um  my question is for uh dr malansky is the  cdc considering ending the practice of  releasing  daily infection accounts for the u.s  given  that there are so many test results that  are not being reported  and are you considering only focusing  on hospitalizations and deaths  thank you yeah thank you for that  question ahmed um  obviously we need to focus not only on  severity of disease and uh importance of  uh our healthcare infrastructure but  also um  case counts are often a leading  indicator because the severity of  disease and healthcare infrastructure  might lag behind what is happening in  case counts so we're thinking through  all of that right now thanks for that  question  next question  let's go to cheyenne haslett at abc news  hi thank you uh this is for jeff i know  the rfp just closed  and exact contracts are still in the  works for uh the 500 million  plan um in january but big picture can  you say the confidence that the rapid  test companies authorized in the us can  actually produce 500 million extra tests  in january for the plan and will those  tests um be coming off of the shelves of  pharmacies at all  no these are additional tests and this  is  made possible by the fact that the fda  across the last few months have  authorized 